---
<ArticleLayout title="Trump Account vs. 529 plan" description="Compare purpose, eligibility, taxes, contributions, investments, control, and withdrawals without reducing the choice to a single winner." sourceIds={['TREASURY-TD-10056','IRS-FORM-4547-INSTRUCTIONS','IRS-529-QA']}>
  <div class="callout"><strong>Short answer:</strong> a Trump Account is structured as a child-owned retirement account with special pre-18 rules; a 529 is built primarily for education. Some families may use both for different goals.</div>
  <ComparisonTable caption="Trump Account and 529 plan: key differences" columns={['Question','Trump Account','529 plan']} rows={rows} />
  <h2>When the Trump Account may be the first action</h2>
  <p>If the child may qualify for the $1,000 pilot contribution, first check auto-account and prior-election status, then review the separate pilot election. A claimed and activated receiving account can also accept family or employer contributions under applicable rules.</p>
  <h2>When a 529 may remain the main savings vehicle</h2>
  <p>Families saving primarily for qualified education expenses may value tax-free qualified withdrawals, broader contribution capacity, and account-owner control. State deductions, credits, fees, and investment menus should be checked plan by plan.</p>
  <h2>Questions to bring to a professional</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>How does your state tax each account?</li>
    <li>How could each account interact with need-based aid or public benefits?</li>
    <li>Who should own or control the money and when should control change?</li>
    <li>What happens if the money is used for a different purpose?</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Use the <a href="/tools/child-savings-account-comparison/">broader account comparison</a> to include UGMA/UTMA, Custodial Roth IRA, and ABLE options.</p>
  <p>For an education-first starting point, read the <a href="/guides/529-plans/">529 plan guide and plan-comparison checklist</a>.</p>
</ArticleLayout>
